# Custom stencil

This tutorial builds a circle that moves by dragging its body and resizes around its centre from a single control. Its implementation uses public geometry and gesture primitives.

<StencilDiagram variant="idea" />

## Register the stencil

Call `getCropperContext()` during component initialization. Register a getter before checking whether cropper state exists. The getter supplies `aspectRatio: 1` and `BoundingBoxType.Circle`; the cropper combines those options with its constraint strategy before the first reset. Registration automatically cleans up on destruction.

The context exposes a stable `cropper` API, reactive `disabled`, and the currently displayed `image`. Read changing values with `$derived`. An enclosing cropper's disabled state must reach every gesture surface.

## Position and draw

`getStencilCoordinates()` supplies layout coordinates to `StencilWrapper`; `getTransitions()` keeps its movement aligned with the image. `StencilOverlay` shades outside the circle. The demo's CSS supplies the round outline and positions the resize control.

<StencilDiagram variant="resize" />

## Move and resize

Two `DraggableArea` instances handle the body and the resize control. They install native non-passive listeners through attachments. The body forwards only the displacement to `moveCoordinates`; forwarding the native event as its second argument would incorrectly put it in the method's options position.

<StencilDiagram variant="directions" />

The resize callback uses the `center` anchor and horizontal displacement for both dimensions. Moving right grows the circle evenly; moving left shrinks it. The registered ratio keeps it square.

<CodeBlock html={stencilSource.html} code={stencilSource.code} />

Each gesture calls its matching completion method: `moveCoordinatesEnd` for the body and `resizeCoordinatesEnd` for the handle. These end interaction state and allow pending reconciliation to run. When composing `BoundingBox` controls instead, preserve the native event in `onMove` forwarding so modifier-key behaviour survives.

## Use it as a child

<Example source={CustomStencilSource} title="CustomStencil" files={{ 'CircleStencil.svelte': CustomStencilFile0 }}>
	<CustomStencil />
</Example>

Drag the circle, then its diagonal control. The cropper can configure core restrictions normally; the custom stencil owns only its presentation and interaction rules. See [stencil context](/docs/composition/stencil-context) for lifecycle details and [Print guides](/docs/tutorials/print-guides) for reactive, product-specific options.
